23:27:15 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: The key is to write like you’re talking to a friend on a noisy, busy day—short, crystal clear, and impossible to misunderstand at a glance. Here’s how to make instructions your crew will actually read and follow: ### 1. Use one-page bullet points, not paragraphs Moving crews are on their feet and often read while holding furniture. Huge blocks of text get skipped. - Print or handwrite in **large, bold font** (at least 18pt). - Put it on a clipboard or tape it to the front door / kitchen counter where they’ll see it immediately. ### 2. Start with the absolute essentials Lead with the two things that cause the most confusion: - **Furniture placement** (e.g., “All bedroom items go to 2nd floor, room at end of hall”) - **What NOT to move** (e.g., “Do not pack anything from the kitchen counter – we’re taking those in our car”) Example opening: > **HELLO! Thank you for your hard work.** > **PLEASE READ BEFORE MOVING ITEMS:** > • Everything labeled “BED 1” goes to the first bedroom on the right upstairs. > • Everything labeled “OFFICE” goes to the basement room with the blue door. > • **DO NOT MOVE** the ladder, plants in the hallway, or the vacuum—those stay. ### 3. Label the house, not just the boxes Even with labeled boxes, someone has to know where “bedroom 2” is. - Stick **large signs** on bedroom doors, bathroom doors, etc.: “BED 1”, “BED 2”, “OFFICE”. - If rooms aren’t obvious (basement office vs. storage), add a short description: “Office with the big window.” ### 4. Use a simple floor plan for furniture placement Draw a rough rectangle for each room on the back of the instruction sheet. Show where the bed, dresser, sofa go using arrows and stick figures. - Don’t worry about scale—just make it obvious which wall they face. - Write “HEADBOARD HERE” with an arrow. Movers love this because it removes all guesswork. ### 5. Highlight fragile and “heavy” items up front Separate a small box on the page for: > **FRAGILE / SPECIAL CARE:** > • The glass dining table top—use the moving blankets inside the red bag by the door. > • The antique dresser in the master bedroom—please carry, don’t drag. ### 6. Communicate “keep-together” sets clearly If something has multiple parts, tape them together or bundle them with a note: > “This bin goes with the bed frame marked BED1—all parts inside.” ### 7. Keep the crew’s humanity in mind Add a friendly line at the top or bottom. Something like: > “Cold water and Gatorade in the cooler on the porch—help yourself. Thanks again!” This isn’t just for kindness; it makes them want to read the whole sheet because they feel respected. ### 8. If you have time: do a 2-minute verbal walkthrough Even the best instructions can’t replace a quick “Here’s the tricky part…” before they start. But if you can’t be there, leave your phone number at the top of the sheet for urgent questions—text is usually best so they can send a photo of an unknown item. **Bottom line:** a single, well-spaced sheet with big labels, arrows, and one clear “do not touch” list will be read. A multi-page memo won’t. Keep it visual, friendly, and so simple they could follow it without speaking English fluently.
